# Product Strategy & OKRs: Meridian Foundations

> Module 1 · Write a Strategy That Can Survive a Test

## 1. Playing to Win cascade

| Question | Your choice |
|---|---|
| **Winning aspiration** | Winning for Meridian's customer means the entire construction organization operates from a connected, accurate, real-time view of project activity, without forcing field teams to take on additional administrative work. |
| **Where to play** | Customers to focus on include: existing enterprise general contractors ($50M+ projects), Mid-market general contractors ($5M-$50M projects). The customers to exclude (for now) include: small contractors, specialty subcontractors, and owners. |
| **How to win** | Meridian's strongest competitive advantage is its existing position as the enterprise system of record combined with the opportunity to become the system of action for field teams. This includes the existing relationships and infrastructure, a connected view of the entire construction lifecycle. |
| **Capabilities required** | To deliver Meridian's How to Win strategy, the team must excel at building an exceptionally simple field experience while maintaining the reliability, integration, and enterprise sophistication of the existing platform. This includes a deep customer understanding and field research, mobile-first product design and usability, enterprise platform and technical execution, and rapid experimentation (test & fail fast). |
| **Management systems** | For Meridian Foundations, I would establish a quarterly strategy scorecard that measures customer adoption, field productivity, enterprise value, and commercial impact.  1. Field Adoption 2. Customer productivity  3. Enterprise platform value - is field adoption making Meridian more valuable? (consider: CSAT, retention, & expansion metrics) |

## 2. Your one hard no

> **Hard no:** We will prioritize field adoption over feature expansion. We will NOT rebuild Meridian into a completely new, mobile-first platform or attempt to replicate every enterprise feature on mobile. Instead, we will build a focused, additive field experience that prioritizes the daily needs of superintendents and foremen: capturing progress, documenting issues, and communicating updates.

## 3. OKR cascade

> **Objective:** Validate and launch a mobile-first field experience that makes it easier for superintendents and foremen to capture, communicate, and manage job-site activity within Meridian.
>
> - **KR1:** Design, test, and validate the proposed MVP experience with target field users. (Target: 80% of usability-test participants successfully complete core workflows without assistance)
> - **KR2:** Complete field research to identify and validate the highest-priority job-site workflows and user pain points. (Target: 15–20 field-user interviews across 3–5 enterprise customers)
> - **KR3:** Develop and release an MVP containing the three prioritized capabilities: field documentation, issue reporting, and enterprise platform integration. (Target: MVP launched within six months).

## 4. AI pressure-test

**Most valid challenge:** AI challenged one major assumption - "we are assuming that field teams don't use Meridian because the product is too complicated, and that a simpler mobile experience will change their behavior." This challenge is fair. The reason the field does not adopt Meridian could be because of friction in the current workflows, lack of perceived value, connectivity issues in the environment in which they work, or even that they are not currently incentivized to use the product in the field.

**What I will change and what I will defend:** I would make one fundamental change: Shift the strategy from "build a simpler mobile experience to drive field adoption" to "identify and eliminate the barriers preventing field teams from using Meridian." To put it simply, the strategy is to make Meridian easier than any workaround. The winning aspiration will also change to: Enable construction field teams to document and communicate job-site activity with less effort than their existing tools, while giving project managers and executives reliable, real-time visibility into project execution.
